---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A desktop reduced to flat gold and black shapes, a tiled window grid and a single status bar sweeping across the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    The customised desktop is the most elaborate method ever devised for not
    doing any work. This course studies it with exactly the seriousness it
    secretly demands.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You will build one desktop. Over the semester you will choose a compositor
    and a window manager, write and version the dotfiles that describe them,
    settle a palette and a typeface, compose a status bar, and photograph the
    result. Then you will do the harder thing: make it reproducible, so that a
    stranger with a bare machine can arrive at the same desktop you did, and
    write an honest account of the hours it cost against the benefit it
    returned.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Students who already live in a terminal and want to think about why, and
    students who have admired a screenshot and wondered what it would take to
    earn one. You should be comfortable on the Linux command line and with git.
    You do not need a design background; you do need a willingness to defend
    your choices out loud, every week, to a room.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title="Lectures" href="/lectures/">
      <p>The twelve-week arc, from the origins of the term to the closing gallery.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly studio: what to bring, what happens, and what leaves the room.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    margin-block: 0 2.5rem;
  }
</style>
